  "prose": {
    "bottom_line": "Investment scams now own 54% of all fraud losses, up 26% year-over-year with median victim loss climbing from $25K to $32K. Recovery pitches surged 336%, re-victimizing the same pool at $15.6M in six months.",
    "key_judgements": [
      "Investment and crypto scams drove $151.4M in losses across six months (54% of total), with robust 26% growth despite flat report volumes.",
      "Recovery pitch losses exploded 336% to $15.6M as median loss per victim more than doubled from $7,550 to $17,575.",
      "Romance scams posted 26% loss growth to $26M with median loss jumping from $20,600 to $30K, signaling deeper victim exploitation.",
      "Text message contact share rose 2.2 percentage points while direct calls fell 1.0 point, suggesting fraudsters are shifting to asynchronous channels that evade real-time intervention."
    ],
    "watch_list": [
      "Deepfakes and AI voice cloning appeared in 6 recent bulletins and could amplify bank investigator impersonation and emergency scams by defeating voice-based authentication and family recognition.",
      "One-time passcode theft tactics (4 bulletins) directly threaten phishing-to-account-takeover scenarios and any control relying on SMS or email verification.",
      "Fake payment notifications via email or text (1 bulletin) enable seller-side overpayment scams and could undermine trust in legitimate Interac e-Transfer alerts."
    ],
    "recommendation_rationales": [
      "Investment scams account for $151.4M (54% of losses) with 26% growth and medium Interac exposure; flagging repeated escalating transfers to crypto on-ramps targets the payment choke-point where victims liquidate savings before losses crystallize.",
      "Recovery pitch losses jumped 336% to $15.6M as fraudsters circle back to a known victim pool; proactive outreach costs little and directly interrupts re-victimization before the second payment leaves the account.",
      "Job scams remain high Interac exposure with $15.8M in losses and a $14,200 median; youth-focused awareness addresses the recruitment funnel before individuals become money mules, reducing downstream transaction risk across all typologies."
    ]
  }
